---
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageContainer from "../../components/PageContainer.astro";
import SectionHeader from "../../components/SectionHeader.astro";
---

<Layout
  pathname={Astro.url.pathname}
  title="Décio D'Almeida — Secret Santa App"
  description="Product design sprint case study: gamifying corporate holiday celebrations with a mobile-first Secret Santa app."
  sections={[
    { name: "Context", id: "contexto" },
    { name: "Challenge", id: "desafio" },
    { name: "Process", id: "processo" },
    { name: "Retrospective", id: "retro" },
  ]}
  lang="en"
>
  <PageContainer>
    <section class="-mt-6">
      <SectionHeader title="Enterprise Engagement: Gamifying Internal Culture for Hybrid Teams" />

      <p class="mt-1 text-lg font-medium text-muted-foreground">
        How I designed, prototyped, and validated a mobile-first gift-exchange app with interactive gamification in a high-intensity, one-week design sprint.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="mt-4">
      <img
        src="/abre_amigo_interno_2.png"
        alt="Secret Santa App"
        class="w-full object-cover rounded-2xl"
      />
    </div>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 id="contexto" class="scroll-mt-20 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Context & The Brief</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-4xl text-pretty text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        As part of the hiring process for Softfocus, I was given a one-week open design challenge: create a digital solution to streamline corporate Secret Santa gift exchanges. The prompt addressed a real operational headache — as corporate teams grow and transition to remote/hybrid setups, holiday gift exchanges become chaotic.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-4xl text-pretty text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        The brief required a complete end-to-end solution addressing: automated drawing mechanics, interactive gift registries, participant profile tracking, mechanics to boost the chance of drawing a specific person, and crowdsourced proposals. Bonus goal: gamification elements — daily quizzes, unlockable hints, and achievements.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-4xl text-pretty text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        My goal was to deliver every single mandatory and bonus feature within a 7-day sprint, from concept to high-fidelity clickable prototype.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 id="desafio" class="scroll-mt-20 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The Challenge</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-4xl text-pretty text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        The briefing required:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-lg text-muted-foreground">
        {[
          "Automated drawing mechanics",
          "Interactive gift registries with store links",
          "Draw details and participant profile tracking",
          "Mechanics to strategically boost the chance of drawing a specific person",
          "Crowdsourced proposals (e.g., voting to adjust the budget)",
          "Gamification: daily quizzes, unlockable hints, achievements",
        ].map((item) => (
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-2 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-foreground/40" />
            {item}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 id="processo" class="scroll-mt-20 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">My Design Process</h2>

      <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold">1. Functional Mapping & Architecture</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-4xl text-pretty text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        To succeed in a high-velocity sprint, I first mapped the requirements into discrete functional modules: matchmaking (automated draw logic with customizable exclusion rules), registry (a visual catalog for store links and price ranges), interaction (private in-app messaging), and gamification (daily micro-challenges to unlock clues). This map formed the navigation architecture and served as the blueprint for my wireframes.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-6">
        <img
          src="/Fluxo_Hierarquia_Info.png"
          alt="Navigation flowchart"
          class="w-full object-cover rounded-2xl"
        />
      </div>

      <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold">2. Dual-User Journeys</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-4xl text-pretty text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        I mapped two distinct operational flows:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-base text-muted-foreground">
        {[
          "The Organizer: Needs seamless group creation, invitation management, rule configuration, and automated draw execution.",
          "The Participant: Demands frictionless entry, rapid registry setup, private chatting, and quick access to gamified activities.",
        ].map((item) => (
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-2 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-foreground/40" />
            {item}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-4xl text-pretty text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        Designing these journeys in parallel ensured that the participant's day-to-day engagement remained as robust as the organizer's administrative dashboard.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <img
          src="/Jornada_Organizador.png"
          alt="Organizer journey"
          class="w-full object-contain rounded-2xl"
        />
        <img
          src="/Jornada_Participante.png"
          alt="Participant journey"
          class="w-full object-contain rounded-2xl"
        />
      </div>

      <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold">3. Competitor Benchmarking</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-4xl text-pretty text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        I reviewed five global holiday-drawing apps. The research revealed that while competitors managed drawing mechanics well, they lacked ongoing user engagement — participants downloaded the app, drew a name, and immediately uninstalled it. This validated my decision to focus heavily on in-app messaging and gamification to drive continuous daily retention leading up to the holiday event.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-6">
        <img
          src="/Benchmark.png"
          alt="Benchmark"
          class="w-full object-contain rounded-2xl"
        />
      </div>

      <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold">4. Brand & UI Design System</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-4xl text-pretty text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        To distance the app from dry corporate utilities, I established a festive, highly engaging design guide:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-base text-muted-foreground">
        {[
          "Visual Metaphor: Interfaces inspired by layered wrapping paper, bows, and holiday patterns.",
          "Color Palette: Premium Deep Slate (#1D3557), Warm Teal (#457B9D), Crimson Red (#E63946), and Winter Mint (#A8DADC).",
          "Typography: Proza Libre for warm, playful headings and Open Sans for highly readable body copy.",
        ].map((item) => (
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-2 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-foreground/40" />
            {item}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <div class="mt-6">
        <img
          src="/Design_Guide.png"
          alt="Design Guide"
          class="w-full object-contain rounded-2xl"
        />
      </div>

      <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold">5. Prototyping & Usability Testing</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-4xl text-pretty text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        I built a fully functional high-fidelity prototype in Figma. On day 6, I ran rapid usability tests with five users. Group creation and the gamified daily quiz were highly intuitive. The gift registry flow was initially confusing — I quickly redesigned the screen to place user-added wishlist items above automated store recommendations, which resolved the bottleneck.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-6">
        <img
          src="/prototipo.png"
          alt="Prototype"
          class="w-full object-contain rounded-2xl"
        />
        <p class="mt-2 text-center text-sm">
          <a
            href="https://www.figma.com/proto/QTHfdzRgwBLMTHhYJfuHLv/Softfocus-Prototipado-Final?node-id=49-532&p=f&t=ihqvqJbbh1Cgzluk-1&scaling=min-zoom&content-scaling=fixed&page-id=0%3A1"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
            class="underline text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors"
          >
            link to full prototype
          </a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14 mb-16">
      <h2 id="retro" class="scroll-mt-20 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Retrospective & Strategic Reflection</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-4xl text-pretty text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        This high-fidelity project was highly successful, serving as the primary baseline that led to my recruitment and subsequent five-year career at Softfocus.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-4xl text-pretty text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        Looking back with years of B2B SaaS experience, I identify two key areas for improvement:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base text-muted-foreground">
        {[
          "Accessibility (WCAG Compliance): The teal color (#457B9D) against a white background falls below the WCAG AA contrast ratio of 4.5:1. Today, I would darken this value or adjust the background to ensure absolute accessibility.",
          "Product Refinement: I would streamline the product registry layout to utilize a more compact card system, making it easier for users to compare and save gift options on smaller mobile screens.",
        ].map((item) => (
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-2 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-foreground/40" />
            {item}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>
  </PageContainer>
</Layout>
